{"requirements":[{"id":"https-8443","requirement":"Serve HTTPS on node1 port 8443.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service was active and enabled, listened on 0.0.0.0:8443, returned a successful HTTPS response, and presented a certificate. The same service, listener, firewall access, and response recovered after node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1ee1175d62b44581","cmd-b45fd93b3f6843d8","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"automatic-pre-expiry-renewal","requirement":"Automatically renew the certificate before it expires.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An enabled, persistent daily timer invokes a renewal unit using a seven-day threshold. With a one-day certificate installed, the renewal unit succeeded and replaced it with a certificate valid for 30 days. The scheduled calendar firing itself was not observed, but its enabled schedule and successful target behavior were directly demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1ee1175d62b44581","cmd-3e6259099ba540de","cmd-b45fd93b3f6843d8"]},{"id":"live-certificate-pickup","requirement":"Pick up each renewed certificate while continuing to answer requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Two bounded rotation tests observed changed peer-certificate fingerprints with an unchanged service PID. All 80 forced-rotation requests and all 60 threshold-renewal requests returned HTTP 200, and the served certificate matched the renewed bundle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-887120c7506e487d","cmd-3e6259099ba540de"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"The final node was package-healthy and had no failed units. State-changing actions were otherwise tied to implementation, correction, bounded rotation evidence, or the required restart protocol. The initial incompatible startup hook briefly caused a service retry loop and was later removed; the corrective action succeeded and no failed-service residue remained. Two evidence files were unnecessarily left in /tmp: /tmp/https-rotation-codes and /tmp/https-auto-renew-codes. These retained files are low-risk residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a159dc8fa74d44de","cmd-0f5e6202d60b4059","cmd-1ccb8397173b4fd9","cmd-887120c7506e487d","cmd-3e6259099ba540de","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. HTTPS service, automatic pre-expiry renewal, and uninterrupted live certificate pickup were directly demonstrated. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete: healthy pre-restart state and acknowledgement were captured, the boot identity changed from 4d842549-003e-4f78-bb26-12732f129e58 to 75ab0437-3619-4f13-a6b5-bfae637c0494, and the service, certificate, timer, firewall, and response recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The daily timer's actual future calendar firing was not awaited; automatic operation is supported by the enabled persistent timer, its recorded schedule, and direct successful execution of its renewal target.","The HTTPS probes used certificate verification bypass because the certificate is self-signed; the task did not require a publicly trusted certificate.","The lifecycle inventory confirms the listener, package health, boot transition, and lack of failed units, but does not independently inventory the installed service and timer files."]}
